<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>If you use your own name as the input to G, explain how many outputs are possible and how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      One possible output. I can only graduate from high school once.

   </para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>If you use your graduation date as the input to P, explain how many outputs are possible and how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Many possible outputs. There are other people who will share the same graduation date as mine - especially my classmates who graduate with me.
   </para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Only one of these two relationships, G or P, is a function. The other is not a function. Which one is the function?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    
    <para>Rule G is a function and Rule P is not.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How do you know the relationship you chose is a function?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      G only assigns one output to the input value.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>For the relationship that is a function, write two input-output pairs from the table using function notation.</item>
