<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    
  
  
  
  </problem></exercise>
<!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>
        Write a rule that describes these input-output pairs:\(F\)(ONE) \(=3\)\(F\)(TWO) \(=3\)\(F\)(THREE) \(=5\)\(F\)(FOUR) \(=4\)

        What rule could define function \(F\)?
     </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    
    <para>Function \(F\) takes a word for its input and gives the number of letters in that word for its output.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>
        Here are some input-output pairs with the same inputs but different outputs:\(v\)(ONE) \(=2\)
        \(v\)(TWO) \(=1\)
        \(v\)(THREE) \(=2\)
        \(v\)(FOUR) \(=2\)

        What rule could define function \(v\)?
     </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    
    <para>Function \(v\) takes a word for its input and gives the number of vowels in that word as its output.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction--></section>
<note class="self-check"><title>Self Check</title>
  <para>The price for pizzas, in dollars, is a function of the number of pizzas bought, \(n\).
    Identify the input, output, and meaning of the statement: \(p(11)=55\)</para></note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
  <section><title>Meanings in Functions </title>
    <para>Review the following examples to help you gain a better understanding of meanings in functions.</para>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <newline/><para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis> </para>
        
        <para>The number of cubic yards of dirt, \(D\), needed to cover a garden with an area of \(a\) square feet is given by \(D=g(a)\).
       </para><para>A garden with area 5000 \(ft^2\) requires 50 \(yd^3\) of dirt. Express this information in terms of the function \(g\).</para>
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> \(g(5000)=50\)
     </para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>Explain the meaning of the statement \(g(100)=1\).</para>
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> The number of cubic yards of dirt required for a garden of 100 square feet is 1. 
     </para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
        
        <para>Let \(f(t)\) be the number of ducks in a lake \(t\) years after 1990. Explain the meaning of each statement:</para>
        
        <para>\(f(5)=30\)
         </para>
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> In 1995 (5 years after 1990), there were 30 ducks in the lake.
     </para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>\(f(10)=40\)</para>
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> In 2000 (10 years after 1990), there were 40 ducks in the lake.
     </para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    <note class="try look-at-me"/>
    <title>Try It: Meanings in Functions</title>
    <para>The amount of garbage, \(G\), produced by a city with population \(p\) is given by \(G=f(p)\). \(G\) is measured in tons per week, and \(p\) is measured in thousands of people.</para>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        <para>The town of Tola has a population of 40,000 and produces 13 tons of garbage each week. Express this information in terms of the function \(f\).</para>
          <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
         </para>
        </problem><solution>
        
        
      
      <para>Compare your answer:</para>
        
        <para>\(13=f(40)\)</para>
      
      </solution></exercise>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        <para>Explain the meaning of the statement \(f(5)=2\).</para>
          <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
         </para>
        </problem><solution>
        
        
      
      <para>Compare your answer:</para>
        
        <para>Five thousand people will produce 2 tons of garbage each week.</para>
      
      </solution></exercise></section>
    </section>
    
